WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:01.933
Kia ora.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:04.417
This is the I N P final, the forecast.

3
00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:07.359
The exam is on Monday the second of November:

4
00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:10.366
forty multi-choice questions at one mark each,

5
00:00:10.366 --> 00:00:13.308
and fifteen short answers at four marks each.

6
00:00:13.308 --> 00:00:17.557
That's sixty marks of short answer against forty of multi-choice,

7
00:00:17.557 --> 00:00:20.695
so the short answers are where this exam is won.

8
00:00:21.145 --> 00:00:26.070
Nobody has posted the real questions, and Lisa sets no practice short answers.

9
00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.543
So every question in this episode is the podcast's own.

10
00:00:29.543 --> 00:00:32.321
But none of them is a guess out of thin air.

11
00:00:32.321 --> 00:00:37.247
Each one comes from something the lecturers pointed at: the Exam Content page,

12
00:00:37.247 --> 00:00:41.477
which lists twenty-nine themes; Lisa's and Janine's revision decks;

13
00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:44.887
the revision session on the twenty-seventh of October,

14
00:00:44.887 --> 00:00:48.486
where Lisa ran quizzes on the topics the class voted for;

15
00:00:48.486 --> 00:00:50.885
and the lecturers' own Canvas quizzes.

16
00:00:50.885 --> 00:00:53.474
Every track starts by saying why it's in.

17
00:00:53.924 --> 00:00:55.033
Here's the order.

18
00:00:55.033 --> 00:01:00.580
First, Lisa's half of the course: social determinants, equity, prevention, screening,

19
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:05.865
primary health care, the Health Strategy, Te Tiriti, the standards of competence,

20
00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:10.107
therapeutic use of self, family-centred care, pain and discharge.

21
00:01:10.107 --> 00:01:15.784
Then Janine's half: the nursing process, holistic assessment, consent, health literacy,

22
00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:21.134
vital signs, documentation, infection control, evidence-based practice, self-care,

23
00:01:21.134 --> 00:01:24.462
long-term conditions, diabetes and rheumatic fever.

24
00:01:24.462 --> 00:01:28.834
Then one track of the one-line facts the multi-choice keeps asking,

25
00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:31.053
and a rapid-fire track at the end.

26
00:01:31.503 --> 00:01:35.318
Now the most useful thing Lisa gave us: her marking guide.

27
00:01:35.318 --> 00:01:40.055
On the twenty-seventh she showed a four-mark practice question on falls.

28
00:01:40.055 --> 00:01:43.476
Part one said identify and describe five strategies.

29
00:01:43.476 --> 00:01:46.304
Part two said explain how they reduce harm.

30
00:01:46.304 --> 00:01:47.949
Her marks went like this.

31
00:01:47.949 --> 00:01:50.186
One mark for identifying the five.

32
00:01:50.186 --> 00:01:52.093
One mark for describing them.

33
00:01:52.093 --> 00:01:54.133
Two marks for the explain part.

34
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:57.027
So the why and the how carry half the marks.

35
00:01:57.027 --> 00:02:00.909
A list of words, on its own, gets you one mark out of four.

36
00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:02.589
Her command words.

37
00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:04.775
Identify means name it, briefly.

38
00:02:04.775 --> 00:02:07.645
Describe means the what: its key features.

39
00:02:07.645 --> 00:02:09.900
Explain means the why or the how.

40
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:14.410
Discuss means go further: examples, both sides, then link it back.

41
00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:18.305
Compare means name both things, then say how they relate.

42
00:02:18.305 --> 00:02:22.747
Bullet points are fine for an identify part, but not for discuss,

43
00:02:22.747 --> 00:02:25.480
because you can't link ideas in bullets.

44
00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.580
And about a hundred and fifty words is a guide, not a count.

45
00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.450
Her words: don't just write two sentences.

46
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:38.029
One more habit from her: use the keywords in the question as the starters of your points.

47
00:02:38.029 --> 00:02:41.775
If the question says falls risk, your first line says falls risk.

48
00:02:42.225 --> 00:02:44.806
In each track, the examiner, that's this voice,

49
00:02:45.256 --> 00:02:46.365
<v Examiner>reads the question,

50
00:02:46.815 --> 00:02:49.597
and then I answer it in the shape you'd write it.

51
00:02:49.597 --> 00:02:53.174
Pause the track after the question if you want to try it first.

52
00:02:53.174 --> 00:02:53.685
Let's go.
